{
  "summary": "Acquires an exclusive file lock on the blocks directory in BlockManager using an RAII helper class. This ensures any consumer of BlockManager, including libbitcoinkernel users and utilities like bitcoin-chainstate, locks the blocks directory for its lifetime.",
  "problem": "Previously, only bitcoind locked the blocks directory during initialization, while the kernel did no directory locking. A kernel program pointed at a running node's blocks directory with a fresh or in-memory database would succeed, open blk00000.dat in place with read/write mode, and silently overwrite existing block files from offset 0, corrupting the running node's data.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: 'with an empty block index BlockManager writes from offset 0 of the node's blk00000.dat, which FlatFileSeq::Open opens in place with \"rb+\". The node keeps running and serves the overwritten bytes as its own blocks.'",
    "Referenced PR #31674 discussion: noted that 'the kernel does not currently do any dir locking, but it should', with a reviewer suggesting moving the lock into the BlockManager constructor."
  ],
